---
import KnockioHomeLayout from "../../layouts/KnockioHomeLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/case-study.css";
---

<KnockioHomeLayout
  title="Case Study: Modern Roofing | Knockio"
  description="How Modern Roofing consolidated five tools onto Knockio, cut operating cost by 75%, and recovered roughly $30,000 a month."
  whiteBg={true}
  exitIntent={false}
>
  <article class="cs">
    <!-- ───────────────────────── Hero ───────────────────────── -->
    <header class="cs-hero">
      <div class="cs-wrap">
        <p class="cs-eyebrow">Case Study · Roofing &amp; Insurance Restoration</p>
        <h1 class="cs-title">
          How Modern Roofing consolidated five tools onto Knockio, cut operating
          cost by 75%, and recovered roughly $30,000 a month
        </h1>
        <p class="cs-dek">
          Five reasonable tools, chosen one at a time, each solving the problem
          in front of it. None of them could see the same homeowner. Here is
          what closing that seam was actually worth.
        </p>

        <dl class="cs-meta">
          <div>
            <dt>Company</dt>
            <dd>Modern Roofing LLC</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Interviewed</dt>
            <dd>Steven Purdy, District Manager</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Footprint</dt>
            <dd>15 locations · 5 states</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Measurement window</dt>
            <dd><mark class="cs-ph">MEASUREMENT WINDOW</mark></dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ──────────────────── Sticky results bar ──────────────────── -->
    <div class="cs-stickybar">
      <div class="cs-wrap cs-stickybar-inner">
        <div class="cs-stat">
          <span class="cs-stat-num">75%</span>
          <span class="cs-stat-label">lower operating cost</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-stat">
          <span class="cs-stat-num">~$30k</span>
          <span class="cs-stat-label">recovered per month</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-stat">
          <span class="cs-stat-num">5 → 2</span>
          <span class="cs-stat-label">software vendors</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-stat">
          <span class="cs-stat-num">6 wks</span>
          <span class="cs-stat-label">kickoff to legacy shutdown</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-body">
      <!-- ───────────────────── At a glance ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="glance">
        <h2 id="glance" class="cs-h2">At a glance</h2>

        <div class="cs-tablewrap">
          <table class="cs-table cs-table--spec">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Founded</th>
                <td>February 4, 2014 — Pelzer, South Carolina</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Footprint</th>
                <td>
                  15 locations across South Carolina, North Carolina, Georgia,
                  Tennessee and Utah
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Volume</th>
                <td>10,600+ roofs completed</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Credentials</th>
                <td>
                  GAF Master Elite — held by fewer than 2% of US contractors;
                  Golden Pledge Warranty
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Model</th>
                <td>
                  Residential and commercial roofing, insurance claim–led,
                  in-house crews, door-to-door canvassing
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Replaced</th>
                <td>
                  Google Voice, standalone canvassing software, separate job
                  management platform, separate time-tracking tool
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Retained</th>
                <td>GoHighLevel — integrated natively, not rebuilt</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Adopted</th>
                <td>
                  Knockio — canvassing, CRM pipeline, media, dispatch, work
                  orders, invoicing, payments and communication in one platform
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-h3">
          Results after <mark class="cs-ph">MEASUREMENT WINDOW</mark>
        </h3>
        <ul class="cs-list cs-list--results">
          <li>
            <strong>75% reduction</strong> in combined software and administrative
            operating cost
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>~$30,000 per month</strong> recovered — approximately
            <strong>$360,000 annualised</strong>
          </li>
          <li>Five vendors reduced to two</li>
          <li>
            Inspection photos available in the estimate at the moment it is
            built, rather than <mark class="cs-ph">X hours</mark> later
          </li>
          <li>
            Rep non-selling admin time cut by roughly
            <mark class="cs-ph">X</mark> hours per week
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── The company ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="company">
        <p class="cs-num">01</p>
        <h2 id="company" class="cs-h2">The company</h2>

        <p>
          Modern Roofing was founded in Pelzer, South Carolina in February 2014
          and has grown from a home office into a 15-location operation spanning
          five states, with more than 10,600 roofs completed. It holds GAF
          Master Elite certification — a credential fewer than 2% of US
          contractors carry — and offers the Golden Pledge Warranty, GAF's
          highest residential warranty.
        </p>
        <p>Two things about how the company operates matter more than its size.</p>

        <p>
          <strong>It runs on insurance claims.</strong> Modern Roofing's stated
          promise to homeowners is a new roof without the insurance headaches:
          the company documents storm damage, submits it to the carrier, and
          attends the adjuster visit. Photographic evidence isn't a nice-to-have
          in that model. It is the product.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It runs its own crews.</strong> Where much of the industry
          subcontracts installation, Modern Roofing employs its crews directly
          and treats paying them fairly and on time as a core commitment. That
          makes labour a real, tracked cost rather than a line item passed
          through to a sub.
        </p>
        <p>
          Steven Purdy manages a district within this structure — which places
          him precisely where canvassing, insurance documentation, production
          and collections are supposed to hand off to one another.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── Before state ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="before">
        <p class="cs-num">02</p>
        <h2 id="before" class="cs-h2">
          The before state: five systems, one homeowner
        </h2>

        <p>
          Modern Roofing had not chosen badly. It had chosen five reasonable
          tools, one at a time, each solving the problem in front of it at that
          moment. The result was five tools that each worked and did not work
          together.
        </p>

        <div class="cs-tablewrap cs-bleed">
          <table class="cs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Tool</th>
                <th scope="col">What it held</th>
                <th scope="col">What it couldn't see</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Google Voice</th>
                <td>Calls and texts, under individual employee accounts</td>
                <td>
                  The customer record, the job, or anyone else's conversation
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">GoHighLevel</th>
                <td>Marketing campaigns and inbound leads</td>
                <td>What happened after the lead was exported</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Canvassing app</th>
                <td>Territories, door pins, inspection photos</td>
                <td>The estimate, the claim, the install, the invoice</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Job management platform</th>
                <td>Scheduling, work orders, invoicing</td>
                <td>Where the lead came from or what was photographed at the door</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Time tracking tool</th>
                <td>Crew hours</td>
                <td>Which job those hours belonged to</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Between each of these sat a person, a spreadsheet, or a Zapier chain.
          The homeowner moved through all five. Their data did not.
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-quote">
          <blockquote>
            A homeowner existed five separate times, in five separate places,
            and none of those versions agreed with each other.
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>
            Steven Purdy<span>District Manager, Modern Roofing</span>
            <mark class="cs-ph cs-ph--quote">Quote pending written approval</mark>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── Claim chain ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="chain">
        <p class="cs-num">03</p>
        <h2 id="chain" class="cs-h2">Where it actually hurt: the claim chain</h2>

        <p>
          Modern Roofing's published process runs Day 1 inspection → Days 2–5
          estimate → Weeks 2–6 claim approval → install day. Following one
          storm-damage job through the old stack shows where the money went.
        </p>

        <ol class="cs-chain">
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Day 1</span>
            <h4>The inspection</h4>
            <p>
              A rep knocks a door in an assigned territory, performs the free
              inspection, and photographs hail impacts, granule loss, missing
              shingles and compromised flashing. Those photos land in the
              canvassing app.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Days 2–5</span>
            <h4>The estimate</h4>
            <p>
              The estimate and carrier documentation are built in the job
              management platform. The photos are in the canvassing app. Someone
              downloads, renames and re-uploads them. Customer name, address,
              phone and scope are retyped by hand. Every job, every time.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">The adjuster visit</span>
            <h4>Assembling the packet</h4>
            <p>
              Modern Roofing attends the adjuster meeting — this is a stated
              differentiator. Doing it well requires having every documented
              image, measurement and note in one place at one time. Assembling
              that packet was manual work performed under time pressure, and
              anything that had not made the jump between systems simply wasn't
              in the room.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Weeks 2–6</span>
            <h4>The waiting</h4>
            <p>
              The longest and most fragile stage. Follow-up during this window
              happened over Google Voice, in individual employees' accounts.
              There was no shared record of what had been said, no automated
              cadence, and nothing to prompt a check-in. Jobs went quiet and
              nobody knew until they were gone.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Install day</span>
            <h4>Working from a copy</h4>
            <p>
              The crew worked from the version of the scope that lived in the
              job platform, which was a retyped copy of what the rep had agreed
              at the door. Discrepancies surfaced on the roof.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Invoicing</span>
            <h4>Two payers, three systems</h4>
            <p>
              The carrier and the homeowner's deductible, sometimes financed at
              0%, reconciled by hand against a job whose full history lived in
              three systems.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Payroll</span>
            <h4>Job costing after the fact</h4>
            <p>
              Crew hours sat in a fourth system with no link to the job, so the
              true labour cost of a roof was a monthly reconciliation exercise
              rather than a fact.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="cs-h3">And then a storm hits</h3>
        <p>
          The site carries a live weather alert banner and promises 24/7
          emergency response with same-day tarping. Modern Roofing's own
          marketing calls it the 72-hour window that matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          A storm produces a surge in GoHighLevel, which has to be moved into the
          canvassing app, allocated across territories, and dispatched — while
          the same office staff are still hand-carrying yesterday's jobs between
          systems. The moment the company most needed to move fast was the
          moment its stack was slowest.
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-quote">
          <blockquote>
            We tell homeowners the first 72 hours after a storm decide the claim.
            We were spending a chunk of those 72 hours moving our own data
            between apps.
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>
            Steven Purdy<span>District Manager, Modern Roofing</span>
            <mark class="cs-ph cs-ph--quote">Quote pending written approval</mark>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── Trigger ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="trigger">
        <p class="cs-num">04</p>
        <h2 id="trigger" class="cs-h2">The trigger</h2>
        <p class="cs-todo">
          <strong>Section incomplete.</strong> Insert the actual trigger — a
          renewal date, the Salt Lake City expansion, a specific storm event
          where the handoff broke, or a quarter of unexplained margin loss. A
          case study without a stated trigger reads as hypothetical; this
          section matters far more than its length.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── Evaluation ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="evaluation">
        <p class="cs-num">05</p>
        <h2 id="evaluation" class="cs-h2">Evaluation</h2>

        <p>Purdy's requirement list was short.</p>

        <ol class="cs-reqs">
          <li>
            Canvassing and job management in the same database — not integrated,
            the <em>same</em>
          </li>
          <li>
            Photos captured at the door usable in the estimate immediately, with
            annotation for adjuster documentation
          </li>
          <li>
            Calls, texts and email attached to the customer record rather than
            to employees
          </li>
          <li>
            Territory and GPS visibility that stayed connected to downstream job
            outcomes
          </li>
          <li>
            Estimates, e-signature, work orders, invoicing and payment in one
            chain
          </li>
          <li>
            Role-based permissions across canvassers, project managers, field
            supervisors, office support and finance
          </li>
          <li>
            Mobile capability strong enough that reps would use it standing on a
            roof
          </li>
          <li>Existing GoHighLevel campaigns preserved, not rebuilt</li>
          <li>No long-term contract</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
          Knockio met all nine in one system. Where ServiceTitan, Jobber and
          Workiz are built primarily around service operations, and SalesRabbit
          and Spotio primarily around canvassing, Knockio holds both sides of
          the same job — which was exactly the seam Modern Roofing was losing
          money in.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two capabilities decided it.
          <strong>Media management with photo annotation</strong> meant
          inspection images could be marked up on site — arrows on lifted
          shingles, notes on granule loss — and stay attached to the lead,
          estimate, work order and claim documentation without being moved
          anywhere. And
          <strong>native GoHighLevel integration</strong> meant the marketing
          engine kept running while leads flowed directly into the pipeline
          instead of being exported.
        </p>
        <p>
          Monthly billing, automatic volume pricing above five users, no setup
          fee and no long-term commitment meant the switch didn't require
          betting the district on it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── Migration ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="migration">
        <p class="cs-num">06</p>
        <h2 id="migration" class="cs-h2">The migration</h2>

        <ol class="cs-chain cs-chain--plan">
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Weeks 1–2</span>
            <h4>Mapping</h4>
            <p>
              Field-by-field mapping from the canvassing app and job platform
              into Knockio's pipeline boards. Boards were built to mirror Modern
              Roofing's actual stages — Inspection, Estimate, Claim Submitted,
              Adjuster Scheduled, Approved, Production, Install, Invoiced,
              Collected — with each stage assigned to the department that owns
              it. <mark class="cs-ph">X</mark> legacy fields were identified as
              unused and retired rather than migrated.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Weeks 2–3</span>
            <h4>Data</h4>
            <p>
              Active customers, open jobs and in-flight claims migrated in full.
              Jobs closed more than <mark class="cs-ph">X months</mark> prior
              were archived to export rather than imported, which cut migration
              volume substantially and eliminated years of accumulated
              duplicates.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Week 3</span>
            <h4>Communication cutover</h4>
            <p>
              The existing business line was ported into Knockio. This is the
              step that ends Google Voice — once the number lives on the
              platform, call and text history attaches to the customer
              automatically with no employee action required.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Weeks 3–4</span>
            <h4>Pilot</h4>
            <p>
              <mark class="cs-ph">PILOT LOCATION</mark> ran Knockio in parallel
              with the legacy stack. The team built stage automations and
              permission roles against live storm jobs rather than a demo.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Week 5</span>
            <h4>Training by role</h4>
            <p>
              Sales reps were trained on mobile only and never shown the desktop
              interface. Office support, project managers, field supervisors and
              finance each received their own session covering only their own
              boards.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cs-chain-when">Week 6</span>
            <h4>Cutover</h4>
            <p>
              GoHighLevel connected via native integration. The four remaining
              tools shut off.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="cs-kicker">
          <mark class="cs-ph">6</mark> weeks from kickoff to legacy shutdown.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── What was difficult ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="difficult">
        <p class="cs-num">07</p>
        <h2 id="difficult" class="cs-h2">What was difficult</h2>

        <p>Two things. Leaving them out would make everything above less believable.</p>

        <p>
          <strong>Reps resisted in week one.</strong> Reps with years of habit in
          the old canvassing app lost selling time relearning an interface. The
          fix was pairing pilot reps with the rest of the team rather than adding
          formal training. <mark class="cs-ph">Confirm actual approach</mark>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Custom reports had to be rebuilt.</strong> Several reports
          leadership depended on didn't port. They were reconstructed within
          <mark class="cs-ph">X weeks</mark> and in most cases came back better,
          because the underlying data now spanned door knock through collected
          payment for the first time. But there was a reporting gap in between,
          and it was uncomfortable.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── After state ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="after">
        <p class="cs-num">08</p>
        <h2 id="after" class="cs-h2">The after state</h2>

        <dl class="cs-after">
          <div>
            <dt>One record, door to payment</dt>
            <dd>
              A lead captured at the door carries its territory, notes, annotated
              photos, appointment, estimate, contract, claim documentation, work
              order, invoice and payment on a single continuous record. Nothing
              is retyped between stages.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Photos where the claim is built</dt>
            <dd>
              Inspection images are captured, annotated and attached on site.
              When the estimate is written days later, the evidence is already
              there. When the adjuster arrives, the packet assembles itself.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Communication on the job, not the phone</dt>
            <dd>
              Calls, texts and emails attach to the customer automatically. Purdy
              can open any job and read its full history without asking anyone.
              When a rep leaves, the relationship stays with the company.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>The 2–6 week gap covered</dt>
            <dd>
              Automated follow-up sequences run through the claim approval
              window. Jobs no longer go quiet because someone forgot.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Storm surge routed, not sorted</dt>
            <dd>
              GoHighLevel leads arrive in the pipeline, get assigned to
              territories and dispatch — inside the 72-hour window rather than
              after it.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Payment collected on site</dt>
            <dd>
              Tap-to-pay and text-to-pay applied to the correct invoice and job
              immediately, across both the deductible and the carrier proceeds.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Labour attached to jobs</dt>
            <dd>
              With time tracking in the same system as work orders, crew hours
              attach to the roof they were spent on. Job-level margin became a
              number instead of a monthly estimate.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <figure class="cs-quote">
          <blockquote>
            The change I didn't expect was in the office. My admin team stopped
            being data-entry clerks and started doing work that actually moves
            jobs forward.
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>
            Steven Purdy<span>District Manager, Modern Roofing</span>
            <mark class="cs-ph cs-ph--quote">Quote pending written approval</mark>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── The numbers ───────────────────── -->
      <section class="cs-wrap cs-section" aria-labelledby="numbers">
        <p class="cs-num">09</p>
        <h2 id="numbers" class="cs-h2">The numbers</h2>
        <p class="cs-note">
          District-level model. Assumptions and the company-wide extrapolation
          are set out in the methodology note below.
        </p>

        <h3 class="cs-h3">Monthly software licensing</h3>
        <div class="cs-tablewrap cs-bleed">
          <table class="cs-table cs-table--fig">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Line item</th>
                <th scope="col" class="cs-r">Before</th>
                <th scope="col" class="cs-r">After</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Google Voice — 60 seats @ $20</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$1,200</td>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">GoHighLevel — retained, integrated</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$497</td>
                <td class="cs-r">$497</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Canvassing platform — 45 seats @ $110</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$4,950</td>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Job management platform — 20 seats @ $149</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$2,980</td>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Time tracking — 60 seats @ $9</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$540</td>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Zapier + standalone e-signature</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$283</td>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="cs-hl">
                <th scope="row">Knockio — 60 users, volume-priced</th>
                <td class="cs-r cs-nil">—</td>
                <td class="cs-r">$2,103</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Total</th>
                <td class="cs-r">$10,450</td>
                <td class="cs-r">$2,600</td>
              </tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-kicker">Licensing reduction: 75%</p>

        <h3 class="cs-h3">Monthly administrative labour</h3>
        <div class="cs-tablewrap cs-bleed">
          <table class="cs-table cs-table--fig">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Activity</th>
                <th scope="col" class="cs-r">Before</th>
                <th scope="col" class="cs-r">After</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  Office duplicate entry and photo transfer
                  <span class="cs-sub">3 staff × 14 hrs/wk @ $29</span>
                </th>
                <td class="cs-r">$5,278</td>
                <td class="cs-r">$1,320</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  Rep non-selling admin
                  <span class="cs-sub">45 reps × 3.5 hrs/wk @ $26</span>
                </th>
                <td class="cs-r">$17,745</td>
                <td class="cs-r">$4,436</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  Manual report assembly
                  <span class="cs-sub">4 managers × 6 hrs/wk @ $48</span>
                </th>
